    shortDescription: "Frequently asked GenerativeAI interview question about Tokens.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTokens are the units a language model processes as input and output. Tokenization is not the same as splitting text into whole words, and token counts affect context and cost.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Tokens accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Tokens are the units a language model processes as input and output. Tokenization is not the same as splitting text into whole words, and token counts affect context and cost.\n2. Use a concrete case: A long support article may consume many input tokens even though it contains fewer human-perceived “words”.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Token budgets must account for prompts, retrieved context, output, and truncation behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Tokens first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA long support article may consume many input tokens even though it contains fewer human-perceived “words”.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use Tokens when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a long support article may consume many input tokens even though it contains fewer human-perceived “words”. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Tokens as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Tokens.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Tokens, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
