    shortDescription: "Frequently asked GenerativeAI interview question about Tokens.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Tokens misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Tokens. Tokens are the units a language model processes as input and output. Tokenization is not the same as splitting text into whole words, and token counts affect context and cost.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Tokens: Tokens are the units a language model processes as input and output. Tokenization is not the same as splitting text into whole words, and token counts affect context and cost.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A long support article may consume many input tokens even though it contains fewer human-perceived “words”.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Token budgets must account for prompts, retrieved context, output, and truncation behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA long support article may consume many input tokens even though it contains fewer human-perceived “words”.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use Tokens when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a long support article may consume many input tokens even though it contains fewer human-perceived “words”. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Tokens as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Tokens.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
