    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA context window is the maximum amount of model input and output context supported for a request under the model’s rules.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Context windows accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: A context window is the maximum amount of model input and output context supported for a request under the model’s rules.\n2. Use a concrete case: A RAG system may have to select and compress relevant documents because the full corpus cannot fit into one request.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Larger context can help with long inputs but can also increase latency and cost and does not guarantee better answers.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Context windows first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA RAG system may have to select and compress relevant documents because the full corpus cannot fit into one request.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use Context windows when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a RAG system may have to select and compress relevant documents because the full corpus cannot fit into one request. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Context windows as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Context windows.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Context windows, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
