    shortDescription: "Frequently asked GenerativeAI interview question about Prompt engineering.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPrompt engineering designs instructions, context, examples, and output constraints to guide a model toward useful behavior.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Prompt engineering accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Prompt engineering designs instructions, context, examples, and output constraints to guide a model toward useful behavior.\n2. Use a concrete case: A classification prompt can define the task, allowed labels, input format, and expected JSON structure.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Prompt tuning alone cannot reliably solve model limitations that require better retrieval, tools, or evaluation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Prompt engineering first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA classification prompt can define the task, allowed labels, input format, and expected JSON structure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use Prompt engineering when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a classification prompt can define the task, allowed labels, input format, and expected JSON structure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Prompt engineering as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Prompt engineering.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Prompt engineering, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
