    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Prompt engineering misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Prompt engineering. Prompt engineering designs instructions, context, examples, and output constraints to guide a model toward useful behavior.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Prompt engineering: Prompt engineering designs instructions, context, examples, and output constraints to guide a model toward useful behavior.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A classification prompt can define the task, allowed labels, input format, and expected JSON structure.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Prompt tuning alone cannot reliably solve model limitations that require better retrieval, tools, or evaluation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA classification prompt can define the task, allowed labels, input format, and expected JSON structure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use Prompt engineering when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a classification prompt can define the task, allowed labels, input format, and expected JSON structure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Prompt engineering as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Prompt engineering.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
