    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPrompt engineering is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Prompt tuning alone cannot reliably solve model limitations that require better retrieval, tools, or evaluation. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Prompt engineering gives you: Prompt engineering designs instructions, context, examples, and output constraints to guide a model toward useful behavior.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Prompt tuning alone cannot reliably solve model limitations that require better retrieval, tools, or evaluation.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A classification prompt can define the task, allowed labels, input format, and expected JSON structure.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA classification prompt can define the task, allowed labels, input format, and expected JSON structure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use Prompt engineering when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a classification prompt can define the task, allowed labels, input format, and expected JSON structure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Prompt engineering as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Prompt engineering.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
