    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nChunking splits source material into retrieval units that can fit model context while retaining enough meaning for retrieval.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Chunking accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Chunking splits source material into retrieval units that can fit model context while retaining enough meaning for retrieval.\n2. Use a concrete case: A long handbook can be split into semantically coherent sections with some overlap where cross-sentence context matters.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Chunks that are too small lose context; chunks that are too large reduce retrieval precision and increase context cost.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Chunking first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA long handbook can be split into semantically coherent sections with some overlap where cross-sentence context matters.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use Chunking when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a long handbook can be split into semantically coherent sections with some overlap where cross-sentence context matters. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Chunking as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Chunking.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Chunking, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
