    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTool calling is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Tool schemas reduce ambiguity, but the application must still authenticate, authorize, validate, and bound the operation. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Tool calling gives you: Tool calling lets a model request an application-defined function with structured arguments so the surrounding system can execute it.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Tool schemas reduce ambiguity, but the application must still authenticate, authorize, validate, and bound the operation.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A model can request `getOrderStatus({orderId})`, after which the application validates and executes the tool call.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA model can request `getOrderStatus({orderId})`, after which the application validates and executes the tool call.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use Tool calling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a model can request `getOrderStatus({orderId})`, after which the application validates and executes the tool call. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Tool calling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Tool calling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
