    shortDescription: "Frequently asked GenerativeAI interview question about LLM security.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLLM security covers risks such as prompt injection, data leakage, unsafe tool use, insecure output handling, and model-specific abuse.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define LLM security accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: LLM security covers risks such as prompt injection, data leakage, unsafe tool use, insecure output handling, and model-specific abuse.\n2. Use a concrete case: A retrieval assistant can treat retrieved text as untrusted data and separately enforce authorization for any tool call.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Security controls can constrain model flexibility, but permissive systems create a larger attack surface.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define LLM security first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA retrieval assistant can treat retrieved text as untrusted data and separately enforce authorization for any tool call.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use LLM security when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a retrieval assistant can treat retrieved text as untrusted data and separately enforce authorization for any tool call. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor LLM security, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
