    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLLM security is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Security controls can constrain model flexibility, but permissive systems create a larger attack surface. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what LLM security gives you: LLM security covers risks such as prompt injection, data leakage, unsafe tool use, insecure output handling, and model-specific abuse.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Security controls can constrain model flexibility, but permissive systems create a larger attack surface.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A retrieval assistant can treat retrieved text as untrusted data and separately enforce authorization for any tool call.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA retrieval assistant can treat retrieved text as untrusted data and separately enforce authorization for any tool call.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use LLM security when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a retrieval assistant can treat retrieved text as untrusted data and separately enforce authorization for any tool call. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
