    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nProduction evaluation combines offline test sets and live signals to measure model quality, safety, cost, and latency after deployment.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Production evaluation accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Production evaluation combines offline test sets and live signals to measure model quality, safety, cost, and latency after deployment.\n2. Use a concrete case: A team can track grounded answer rate, user correction rate, latency, and cost while also running a fixed regression set before releases.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Online metrics can be noisy or biased, so they should complement controlled evaluation rather than replace it.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Production evaluation first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA team can track grounded answer rate, user correction rate, latency, and cost while also running a fixed regression set before releases.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use Production evaluation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a team can track grounded answer rate, user correction rate, latency, and cost while also running a fixed regression set before releases. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Production evaluation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Production evaluation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Production evaluation, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
