    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Production evaluation in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A team can track grounded answer rate, user correction rate, latency, and cost while also running a fixed regression set before releases.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Production evaluation combines offline test sets and live signals to measure model quality, safety, cost, and latency after deployment.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A team can track grounded answer rate, user correction rate, latency, and cost while also running a fixed regression set before releases.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Online metrics can be noisy or biased, so they should complement controlled evaluation rather than replace it.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA team can track grounded answer rate, user correction rate, latency, and cost while also running a fixed regression set before releases.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use Production evaluation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a team can track grounded answer rate, user correction rate, latency, and cost while also running a fixed regression set before releases. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Production evaluation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Production evaluation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
