    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Production evaluation when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Production evaluation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Production evaluation combines offline test sets and live signals to measure model quality, safety, cost, and latency after deployment.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A team can track grounded answer rate, user correction rate, latency, and cost while also running a fixed regression set before releases.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Online metrics can be noisy or biased, so they should complement controlled evaluation rather than replace it.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA team can track grounded answer rate, user correction rate, latency, and cost while also running a fixed regression set before releases.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn ai-powered application may use Production evaluation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a team can track grounded answer rate, user correction rate, latency, and cost while also running a fixed regression set before releases. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Production evaluation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Production evaluation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
