    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Data Science fundamentals.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nData science combines statistics, programming, domain knowledge, and data analysis to answer questions or build predictive systems.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Data Science fundamentals accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Data science combines statistics, programming, domain knowledge, and data analysis to answer questions or build predictive systems.\n2. Use a concrete case: A retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: A technically strong model is not useful if the data or business question is wrong.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Data Science fundamentals first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data Science fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data Science fundamentals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data Science fundamentals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Data Science fundamentals, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
