    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Data Science fundamentals is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Data science combines statistics, programming, domain knowledge, and data analysis to answer questions or build predictive systems.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Data science combines statistics, programming, domain knowledge, and data analysis to answer questions or build predictive systems.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: A technically strong model is not useful if the data or business question is wrong.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data Science fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data Science fundamentals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data Science fundamentals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
