    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Data Science fundamentals when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Data science combines statistics, programming, domain knowledge, and data analysis to answer questions or build predictive systems. A concrete application is A retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Data Science fundamentals provides the required behavior: Data science combines statistics, programming, domain knowledge, and data analysis to answer questions or build predictive systems.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: A technically strong model is not useful if the data or business question is wrong.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Data Science fundamentals fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data Science fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data Science fundamentals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data Science fundamentals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
