    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Data Science fundamentals.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Data Science fundamentals misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Data Science fundamentals. Data science combines statistics, programming, domain knowledge, and data analysis to answer questions or build predictive systems.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Data Science fundamentals: Data science combines statistics, programming, domain knowledge, and data analysis to answer questions or build predictive systems.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A technically strong model is not useful if the data or business question is wrong.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data Science fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data Science fundamentals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data Science fundamentals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Data Science fundamentals misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Data Science fundamentals. Data science combines statistics, programming, domain knowledge, and data analysis to answer questions or build predictive systems.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Data Science fundamentals: Data science combines statistics, programming, domain knowledge, and data analysis to answer questions or build predictive systems.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A technically strong model is not useful if the data or business question is wrong.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data Science fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data Science fundamentals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data Science fundamentals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
