    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Data Science fundamentals.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nData Science fundamentals is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. A technically strong model is not useful if the data or business question is wrong. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Data Science fundamentals gives you: Data science combines statistics, programming, domain knowledge, and data analysis to answer questions or build predictive systems.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: A technically strong model is not useful if the data or business question is wrong.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data Science fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data Science fundamentals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data Science fundamentals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
