    slug: "datascience-what-advanced-follow-up-question-could-be-asked-about-data-science-fundamentals-10",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Data Science fundamentals.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Data Science fundamentals when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Data Science fundamentals.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Data science combines statistics, programming, domain knowledge, and data analysis to answer questions or build predictive systems.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: A technically strong model is not useful if the data or business question is wrong.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data Science fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a retail team can use customer and transaction data to estimate demand and identify drivers of churn. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data Science fundamentals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data Science fundamentals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
