    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Data lifecycle.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe data lifecycle covers collection, validation, storage, transformation, analysis, use, and eventual retention or deletion.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Data lifecycle accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: The data lifecycle covers collection, validation, storage, transformation, analysis, use, and eventual retention or deletion.\n2. Use a concrete case: A feature pipeline can ingest events, validate them, store raw data, transform it for training, and archive or delete data according to policy.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: More retained data can improve history but increases cost, governance, and privacy burden.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Data lifecycle first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA feature pipeline can ingest events, validate them, store raw data, transform it for training, and archive or delete data according to policy.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data lifecycle when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a feature pipeline can ingest events, validate them, store raw data, transform it for training, and archive or delete data according to policy. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data lifecycle as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data lifecycle.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Data lifecycle, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
