    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Data lifecycle.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Data lifecycle misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Data lifecycle. The data lifecycle covers collection, validation, storage, transformation, analysis, use, and eventual retention or deletion.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Data lifecycle: The data lifecycle covers collection, validation, storage, transformation, analysis, use, and eventual retention or deletion.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A feature pipeline can ingest events, validate them, store raw data, transform it for training, and archive or delete data according to policy.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: More retained data can improve history but increases cost, governance, and privacy burden.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA feature pipeline can ingest events, validate them, store raw data, transform it for training, and archive or delete data according to policy.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data lifecycle when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a feature pipeline can ingest events, validate them, store raw data, transform it for training, and archive or delete data according to policy. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data lifecycle as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data lifecycle.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
