    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Data lifecycle.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Data lifecycle when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Data lifecycle.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: The data lifecycle covers collection, validation, storage, transformation, analysis, use, and eventual retention or deletion.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A feature pipeline can ingest events, validate them, store raw data, transform it for training, and archive or delete data according to policy.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: More retained data can improve history but increases cost, governance, and privacy burden.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA feature pipeline can ingest events, validate them, store raw data, transform it for training, and archive or delete data according to policy.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data lifecycle when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a feature pipeline can ingest events, validate them, store raw data, transform it for training, and archive or delete data according to policy. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data lifecycle as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data lifecycle.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
