    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Data quality.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nData quality describes whether data is accurate, complete, consistent, timely, valid, and fit for its intended use.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Data quality accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Data quality describes whether data is accurate, complete, consistent, timely, valid, and fit for its intended use.\n2. Use a concrete case: A duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Quality checks catch problems, but business owners still need to define what “correct” means.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Data quality first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data quality when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data quality as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data quality.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Data quality, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
