    question: "How would you troubleshoot a problem involving Data quality?",
    slug: "datascience-how-would-you-troubleshoot-a-problem-involving-data-quality-27",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Data quality.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Data quality misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Data quality. Data quality describes whether data is accurate, complete, consistent, timely, valid, and fit for its intended use.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Data quality: Data quality describes whether data is accurate, complete, consistent, timely, valid, and fit for its intended use.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Quality checks catch problems, but business owners still need to define what “correct” means.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data quality when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data quality as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data quality.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Data quality misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Data quality. Data quality describes whether data is accurate, complete, consistent, timely, valid, and fit for its intended use.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Data quality: Data quality describes whether data is accurate, complete, consistent, timely, valid, and fit for its intended use.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Quality checks catch problems, but business owners still need to define what “correct” means.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data quality when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data quality as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data quality.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
