    question: "What are the main trade-offs of Data quality?",
    slug: "datascience-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-data-quality-28",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Data quality.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nData quality is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Quality checks catch problems, but business owners still need to define what “correct” means. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Data quality gives you: Data quality describes whether data is accurate, complete, consistent, timely, valid, and fit for its intended use.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Quality checks catch problems, but business owners still need to define what “correct” means.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data quality when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data quality as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data quality.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
