    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Data quality.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Data quality when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Data quality.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Data quality describes whether data is accurate, complete, consistent, timely, valid, and fit for its intended use.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Quality checks catch problems, but business owners still need to define what “correct” means.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data quality when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data quality as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data quality.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Data quality when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Data quality.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Data quality describes whether data is accurate, complete, consistent, timely, valid, and fit for its intended use.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Quality checks catch problems, but business owners still need to define what “correct” means.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Data quality when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a duplicate customer record can distort counts and model features even if every individual field has a valid type. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Data quality as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Data quality.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
