    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about EDA.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nExploratory data analysis uses summaries and visualizations to understand distributions, missingness, relationships, and anomalies before formal modeling.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define EDA accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Exploratory data analysis uses summaries and visualizations to understand distributions, missingness, relationships, and anomalies before formal modeling.\n2. Use a concrete case: Plotting order values by day can reveal skew, seasonality, and outliers that an average hides.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: EDA can guide hypotheses, but patterns found by exploration still need validation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define EDA first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nPlotting order values by day can reveal skew, seasonality, and outliers that an average hides.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use EDA when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, plotting order values by day can reveal skew, seasonality, and outliers that an average hides. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating EDA as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with EDA.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor EDA, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
