    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDescriptive statistics summarize observed data using measures such as mean, median, spread, and quantiles.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Descriptive statistics accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Descriptive statistics summarize observed data using measures such as mean, median, spread, and quantiles.\n2. Use a concrete case: The median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Summary statistics can hide multimodal or subgroup-specific behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Descriptive statistics first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nThe median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Descriptive statistics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, the median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Descriptive statistics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Descriptive statistics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Descriptive statistics, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
