    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Descriptive statistics is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Descriptive statistics summarize observed data using measures such as mean, median, spread, and quantiles.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Descriptive statistics summarize observed data using measures such as mean, median, spread, and quantiles.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: The median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Summary statistics can hide multimodal or subgroup-specific behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nThe median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Descriptive statistics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, the median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Descriptive statistics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Descriptive statistics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Descriptive statistics is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Descriptive statistics summarize observed data using measures such as mean, median, spread, and quantiles.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Descriptive statistics summarize observed data using measures such as mean, median, spread, and quantiles.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: The median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Summary statistics can hide multimodal or subgroup-specific behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nThe median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Descriptive statistics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, the median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Descriptive statistics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Descriptive statistics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
