    slug: "datascience-what-are-the-main-benefits-of-descriptive-statistics-44",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Descriptive statistics.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Descriptive statistics comes from the behavior it provides: Descriptive statistics summarize observed data using measures such as mean, median, spread, and quantiles. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: The median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Descriptive statistics summarize observed data using measures such as mean, median, spread, and quantiles.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: The median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n3. State the trade-off: Summary statistics can hide multimodal or subgroup-specific behavior.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nThe median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Descriptive statistics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, the median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Descriptive statistics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Descriptive statistics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
