    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Descriptive statistics.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Descriptive statistics misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Descriptive statistics. Descriptive statistics summarize observed data using measures such as mean, median, spread, and quantiles.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Descriptive statistics: Descriptive statistics summarize observed data using measures such as mean, median, spread, and quantiles.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: The median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Summary statistics can hide multimodal or subgroup-specific behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nThe median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Descriptive statistics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, the median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Descriptive statistics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Descriptive statistics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Descriptive statistics misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Descriptive statistics. Descriptive statistics summarize observed data using measures such as mean, median, spread, and quantiles.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Descriptive statistics: Descriptive statistics summarize observed data using measures such as mean, median, spread, and quantiles.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: The median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Summary statistics can hide multimodal or subgroup-specific behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nThe median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Descriptive statistics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, the median order value may describe a skewed transaction dataset better than the mean alone. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Descriptive statistics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Descriptive statistics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
