    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nProbability quantifies uncertainty about outcomes and events using a defined sample space or probabilistic model.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Probability accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Probability quantifies uncertainty about outcomes and events using a defined sample space or probabilistic model.\n2. Use a concrete case: A classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Probabilities are model-dependent and should not be treated as certainty.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Probability first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Probability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Probability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Probability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Probability, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
