    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Probability are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Probability quantifies uncertainty about outcomes and events using a defined sample space or probabilistic model.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating Probability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Probability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: A classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nA classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Probability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Probability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Probability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Probability. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
