    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Probability when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Probability quantifies uncertainty about outcomes and events using a defined sample space or probabilistic model. A concrete application is A classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Probability provides the required behavior: Probability quantifies uncertainty about outcomes and events using a defined sample space or probabilistic model.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Probabilities are model-dependent and should not be treated as certainty.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Probability fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Probability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Probability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Probability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
