    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Probability when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Probability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Probability quantifies uncertainty about outcomes and events using a defined sample space or probabilistic model.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Probabilities are model-dependent and should not be treated as certainty.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Probability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a classifier can estimate the probability that an email belongs to a spam category. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Probability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Probability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
