    question: "What are the main trade-offs of Distributions?",
    slug: "datascience-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-distributions-68",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Distributions.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDistributions is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Choosing a convenient distribution without checking fit can produce misleading conclusions. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Distributions gives you: A probability distribution describes how values or outcomes are distributed. Common distributions model continuous or discrete variables with different shapes and assumptions.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Choosing a convenient distribution without checking fit can produce misleading conclusions.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A service latency distribution may be right-skewed, making percentiles more informative than an average.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA service latency distribution may be right-skewed, making percentiles more informative than an average.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Distributions when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service latency distribution may be right-skewed, making percentiles more informative than an average. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Distributions as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Distributions.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
