    slug: "datascience-what-is-sampling-71",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Sampling.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSampling selects a subset of a population for analysis when measuring the entire population is impractical.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Sampling accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Sampling selects a subset of a population for analysis when measuring the entire population is impractical.\n2. Use a concrete case: A survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Sampling bias can make a large sample unrepresentative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Sampling first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Sampling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Sampling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Sampling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Sampling, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSampling selects a subset of a population for analysis when measuring the entire population is impractical.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Sampling accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Sampling selects a subset of a population for analysis when measuring the entire population is impractical.\n2. Use a concrete case: A survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Sampling bias can make a large sample unrepresentative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Sampling first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Sampling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Sampling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Sampling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Sampling, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
