    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Sampling.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Sampling is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Sampling selects a subset of a population for analysis when measuring the entire population is impractical.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Sampling selects a subset of a population for analysis when measuring the entire population is impractical.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Sampling bias can make a large sample unrepresentative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Sampling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Sampling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Sampling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
