    slug: "datascience-how-would-you-explain-sampling-in-an-interview-79",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Sampling.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Sampling in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Sampling selects a subset of a population for analysis when measuring the entire population is impractical.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Sampling bias can make a large sample unrepresentative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Sampling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Sampling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Sampling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
