    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Sampling.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Sampling when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Sampling.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Sampling selects a subset of a population for analysis when measuring the entire population is impractical.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Sampling bias can make a large sample unrepresentative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Sampling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Sampling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Sampling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Sampling when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Sampling.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Sampling selects a subset of a population for analysis when measuring the entire population is impractical.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Sampling bias can make a large sample unrepresentative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Sampling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a survey can sample users while stratifying by region to avoid overrepresenting one segment. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Sampling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Sampling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
