    slug: "datascience-what-is-correlation-81",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Correlation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCorrelation measures the degree to which two variables move together according to a defined statistic.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Correlation accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Correlation measures the degree to which two variables move together according to a defined statistic.\n2. Use a concrete case: Higher ad spend and higher sales may correlate, but that alone does not show that ad spend caused the increase.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Correlation can be distorted by confounding variables, outliers, or nonlinear relationships.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Correlation first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nHigher ad spend and higher sales may correlate, but that alone does not show that ad spend caused the increase.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Correlation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, higher ad spend and higher sales may correlate, but that alone does not show that ad spend caused the increase. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Correlation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Correlation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Correlation, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
