    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Correlation are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Correlation measures the degree to which two variables move together according to a defined statistic.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating Correlation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Correlation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: Higher ad spend and higher sales may correlate, but that alone does not show that ad spend caused the increase.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nHigher ad spend and higher sales may correlate, but that alone does not show that ad spend caused the increase.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Correlation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, higher ad spend and higher sales may correlate, but that alone does not show that ad spend caused the increase. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Correlation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Correlation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Correlation. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
