    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Correlation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Correlation when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Correlation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Correlation measures the degree to which two variables move together according to a defined statistic.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Higher ad spend and higher sales may correlate, but that alone does not show that ad spend caused the increase.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Correlation can be distorted by confounding variables, outliers, or nonlinear relationships.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nHigher ad spend and higher sales may correlate, but that alone does not show that ad spend caused the increase.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Correlation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, higher ad spend and higher sales may correlate, but that alone does not show that ad spend caused the increase. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Correlation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Correlation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
