    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Causation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCausation is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Causal claims require stronger design or assumptions than ordinary correlation. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Causation gives you: Causation means a change in one factor produces a change in another under a specified causal framework.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Causal claims require stronger design or assumptions than ordinary correlation.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A randomized experiment can test whether showing a new checkout flow changes conversion.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA randomized experiment can test whether showing a new checkout flow changes conversion.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Causation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a randomized experiment can test whether showing a new checkout flow changes conversion. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Causation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Causation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
