    question: "What is Hypothesis testing?",
    slug: "datascience-what-is-hypothesis-testing-101",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Hypothesis testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Hypothesis testing accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n2. Use a concrete case: A product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: A p-value alone does not measure business importance or prove a hypothesis true.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Hypothesis testing first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Hypothesis testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Hypothesis testing, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Hypothesis testing accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n2. Use a concrete case: A product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: A p-value alone does not measure business importance or prove a hypothesis true.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Hypothesis testing first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Hypothesis testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Hypothesis testing, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
