    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Hypothesis testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: a product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Hypothesis testing addresses it: Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: A product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: A p-value alone does not measure business importance or prove a hypothesis true.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nA product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Hypothesis testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
