    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Hypothesis testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Hypothesis testing is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: A p-value alone does not measure business importance or prove a hypothesis true.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Hypothesis testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Hypothesis testing is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: A p-value alone does not measure business importance or prove a hypothesis true.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Hypothesis testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
