    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Hypothesis testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Hypothesis testing are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: A product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nA product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Hypothesis testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Hypothesis testing. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
