    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Hypothesis testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Hypothesis testing misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Hypothesis testing. Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Hypothesis testing: Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A p-value alone does not measure business importance or prove a hypothesis true.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Hypothesis testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Hypothesis testing misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Hypothesis testing. Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Hypothesis testing: Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A p-value alone does not measure business importance or prove a hypothesis true.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Hypothesis testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
