    slug: "datascience-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-hypothesis-testing-108",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Hypothesis testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHypothesis testing is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. A p-value alone does not measure business importance or prove a hypothesis true. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Hypothesis testing gives you: Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: A p-value alone does not measure business importance or prove a hypothesis true.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Hypothesis testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
