    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you keep Hypothesis testing reliable as the product and test suite grow?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Hypothesis testing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Hypothesis testing compares observed data with what would be expected under a null hypothesis using a specified test and decision rule.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: A p-value alone does not measure business importance or prove a hypothesis true.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Hypothesis testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product team can test whether a new ranking algorithm changes average conversion beyond normal variation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
