    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Confidence intervals.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA confidence interval gives a range produced by a statistical procedure that captures uncertainty in an estimated quantity.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Confidence intervals accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: A confidence interval gives a range produced by a statistical procedure that captures uncertainty in an estimated quantity.\n2. Use a concrete case: A conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Interval interpretation depends on the underlying assumptions and sampling procedure.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Confidence intervals first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Confidence intervals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Confidence intervals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Confidence intervals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Confidence intervals, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
