    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Confidence intervals.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Confidence intervals is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. A confidence interval gives a range produced by a statistical procedure that captures uncertainty in an estimated quantity.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: A confidence interval gives a range produced by a statistical procedure that captures uncertainty in an estimated quantity.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Interval interpretation depends on the underlying assumptions and sampling procedure.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Confidence intervals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Confidence intervals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Confidence intervals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Confidence intervals is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. A confidence interval gives a range produced by a statistical procedure that captures uncertainty in an estimated quantity.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: A confidence interval gives a range produced by a statistical procedure that captures uncertainty in an estimated quantity.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Interval interpretation depends on the underlying assumptions and sampling procedure.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Confidence intervals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Confidence intervals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Confidence intervals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
