    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Confidence intervals when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Confidence intervals.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: A confidence interval gives a range produced by a statistical procedure that captures uncertainty in an estimated quantity.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Interval interpretation depends on the underlying assumptions and sampling procedure.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Confidence intervals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Confidence intervals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Confidence intervals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Confidence intervals when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Confidence intervals.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: A confidence interval gives a range produced by a statistical procedure that captures uncertainty in an estimated quantity.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Interval interpretation depends on the underlying assumptions and sampling procedure.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Confidence intervals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a conversion-rate estimate can be reported with an interval rather than only a point estimate. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Confidence intervals as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Confidence intervals.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
