    slug: "datascience-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-regression-128",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Regression.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRegression is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. A good fit on training data can still generalize poorly because of leakage, overfitting, or distribution shift. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Regression gives you: Regression models a target as a function of one or more predictors, often for continuous outcomes.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: A good fit on training data can still generalize poorly because of leakage, overfitting, or distribution shift.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: When authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Regression when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when authentication changes, run tests covering login, logout, password reset, session expiry, and authorization paths that may be affected. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
