    slug: "datascience-what-is-classification-131",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Classification.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nClassification predicts one or more discrete classes from input features.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Classification accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Classification predicts one or more discrete classes from input features.\n2. Use a concrete case: A support system can classify tickets into billing, technical, and account categories.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Class imbalance and threshold choice can make accuracy misleading.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Classification first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA support system can classify tickets into billing, technical, and account categories.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Classification when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a support system can classify tickets into billing, technical, and account categories. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Classification as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Classification.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Classification, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
