    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Classification.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nClassification is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Class imbalance and threshold choice can make accuracy misleading. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Classification gives you: Classification predicts one or more discrete classes from input features.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Class imbalance and threshold choice can make accuracy misleading.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A support system can classify tickets into billing, technical, and account categories.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA support system can classify tickets into billing, technical, and account categories.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Classification when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a support system can classify tickets into billing, technical, and account categories. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Classification as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Classification.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
