    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Classification when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Classification.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Classification predicts one or more discrete classes from input features.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A support system can classify tickets into billing, technical, and account categories.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Class imbalance and threshold choice can make accuracy misleading.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA support system can classify tickets into billing, technical, and account categories.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Classification when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a support system can classify tickets into billing, technical, and account categories. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Classification as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Classification.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
