    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because decision trees split data recursively using feature conditions to make predictions. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: represent folders or organizational structures as parent-child nodes in a tree.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Trees addresses it: Decision trees split data recursively using feature conditions to make predictions.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: Represent folders or organizational structures as parent-child nodes in a tree.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Deep trees can overfit; pruning or ensembles can improve generalization.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nRepresent folders or organizational structures as parent-child nodes in a tree.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Trees when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, represent folders or organizational structures as parent-child nodes in a tree. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Trees as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Trees.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision."