    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Trees.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Trees is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Decision trees split data recursively using feature conditions to make predictions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Decision trees split data recursively using feature conditions to make predictions.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: Represent folders or organizational structures as parent-child nodes in a tree.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Deep trees can overfit; pruning or ensembles can improve generalization.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nRepresent folders or organizational structures as parent-child nodes in a tree.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Trees when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, represent folders or organizational structures as parent-child nodes in a tree. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Trees as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Trees.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
