    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Trees when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Trees.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Decision trees split data recursively using feature conditions to make predictions.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Represent folders or organizational structures as parent-child nodes in a tree.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Deep trees can overfit; pruning or ensembles can improve generalization.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nRepresent folders or organizational structures as parent-child nodes in a tree.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Trees when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, represent folders or organizational structures as parent-child nodes in a tree. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Trees as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Trees.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
