    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nEnsemble methods combine multiple models to improve predictive performance, stability, or robustness.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Ensembles accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Ensemble methods combine multiple models to improve predictive performance, stability, or robustness.\n2. Use a concrete case: Random forests combine many decision trees trained on varied samples and features.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Ensembles can improve accuracy but often cost more compute and reduce simple interpretability.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Ensembles first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nRandom forests combine many decision trees trained on varied samples and features.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Ensembles when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, random forests combine many decision trees trained on varied samples and features. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Ensembles as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Ensembles.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Ensembles, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
