    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Clustering misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Clustering. Clustering groups data points according to a similarity objective without requiring labeled target classes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Clustering: Clustering groups data points according to a similarity objective without requiring labeled target classes.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A retailer can cluster customers by purchasing behavior to explore segments.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Clusters depend on representation and distance assumptions and do not automatically correspond to meaningful business categories.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA retailer can cluster customers by purchasing behavior to explore segments.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Clustering when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a retailer can cluster customers by purchasing behavior to explore segments. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Clustering as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Clustering.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Clustering misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Clustering. Clustering groups data points according to a similarity objective without requiring labeled target classes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Clustering: Clustering groups data points according to a similarity objective without requiring labeled target classes.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A retailer can cluster customers by purchasing behavior to explore segments.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Clusters depend on representation and distance assumptions and do not automatically correspond to meaningful business categories.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA retailer can cluster customers by purchasing behavior to explore segments.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Clustering when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a retailer can cluster customers by purchasing behavior to explore segments. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Clustering as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Clustering.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
