    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Feature engineering is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Feature engineering transforms raw inputs into representations that help a model learn the target relationship.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Feature engineering transforms raw inputs into representations that help a model learn the target relationship.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A transaction timestamp can be transformed into day-of-week and hour features when purchasing behavior varies by time.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Features can leak future information if their computation uses data unavailable at prediction time.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA transaction timestamp can be transformed into day-of-week and hour features when purchasing behavior varies by time.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Feature engineering when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a transaction timestamp can be transformed into day-of-week and hour features when purchasing behavior varies by time. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Feature engineering as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Feature engineering.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Feature engineering is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Feature engineering transforms raw inputs into representations that help a model learn the target relationship.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Feature engineering transforms raw inputs into representations that help a model learn the target relationship.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A transaction timestamp can be transformed into day-of-week and hour features when purchasing behavior varies by time.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Features can leak future information if their computation uses data unavailable at prediction time.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA transaction timestamp can be transformed into day-of-week and hour features when purchasing behavior varies by time.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Feature engineering when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a transaction timestamp can be transformed into day-of-week and hour features when purchasing behavior varies by time. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Feature engineering as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Feature engineering.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
