    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Model validation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nModel validation estimates how a trained model will perform on unseen data using holdout sets or cross-validation as appropriate.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Model validation accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Model validation estimates how a trained model will perform on unseen data using holdout sets or cross-validation as appropriate.\n2. Use a concrete case: A classification model can be trained on one split and evaluated on a separate test set that was not used for model selection.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Repeated tuning against the test set leaks information and weakens the estimate of generalization.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Model validation first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA classification model can be trained on one split and evaluated on a separate test set that was not used for model selection.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Model validation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a classification model can be trained on one split and evaluated on a separate test set that was not used for model selection. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Model validation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Model validation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Model validation, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
