    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Model validation is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Model validation estimates how a trained model will perform on unseen data using holdout sets or cross-validation as appropriate.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Model validation estimates how a trained model will perform on unseen data using holdout sets or cross-validation as appropriate.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A classification model can be trained on one split and evaluated on a separate test set that was not used for model selection.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Repeated tuning against the test set leaks information and weakens the estimate of generalization.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA classification model can be trained on one split and evaluated on a separate test set that was not used for model selection.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Model validation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a classification model can be trained on one split and evaluated on a separate test set that was not used for model selection. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Model validation as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Model validation.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
