    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Metrics.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMetrics quantify model performance using measures chosen for the task, such as precision, recall, F1, MAE, or ROC-AUC.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Metrics accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Metrics quantify model performance using measures chosen for the task, such as precision, recall, F1, MAE, or ROC-AUC.\n2. Use a concrete case: For fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: No single metric captures every business consequence; thresholds and error costs matter.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Metrics first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Metrics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Metrics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Metrics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Metrics, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
