    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Metrics is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Metrics quantify model performance using measures chosen for the task, such as precision, recall, F1, MAE, or ROC-AUC.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Metrics quantify model performance using measures chosen for the task, such as precision, recall, F1, MAE, or ROC-AUC.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: For fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: No single metric captures every business consequence; thresholds and error costs matter.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nFor fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Metrics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Metrics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Metrics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
