    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Metrics.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Metrics comes from the behavior it provides: Metrics quantify model performance using measures chosen for the task, such as precision, recall, F1, MAE, or ROC-AUC. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: For fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Metrics quantify model performance using measures chosen for the task, such as precision, recall, F1, MAE, or ROC-AUC.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: For fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly.\n3. State the trade-off: No single metric captures every business consequence; thresholds and error costs matter.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nFor fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Metrics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Metrics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Metrics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
