    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Metrics.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Metrics misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Metrics. Metrics quantify model performance using measures chosen for the task, such as precision, recall, F1, MAE, or ROC-AUC.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Metrics: Metrics quantify model performance using measures chosen for the task, such as precision, recall, F1, MAE, or ROC-AUC.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: For fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: No single metric captures every business consequence; thresholds and error costs matter.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nFor fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Metrics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Metrics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Metrics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
