    shortDescription: "Frequently asked DataScience interview question about Metrics.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMetrics is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. No single metric captures every business consequence; thresholds and error costs matter. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Metrics gives you: Metrics quantify model performance using measures chosen for the task, such as precision, recall, F1, MAE, or ROC-AUC.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: No single metric captures every business consequence; thresholds and error costs matter.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: For fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nFor fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA data analysis or machine-learning workflow may use Metrics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for fraud detection, recall may matter more than raw accuracy if missing fraud is costly. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Metrics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Metrics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
