    question: "What is API fundamentals?",
    slug: "api-webservice-what-is-api-fundamentals-1",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about API fundamentals.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn API is a defined interface through which software components exchange data or trigger operations. A good API contract specifies inputs, outputs, errors, authentication requirements, and compatibility expectations.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define API fundamentals accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: An API is a defined interface through which software components exchange data or trigger operations. A good API contract specifies inputs, outputs, errors, authentication requirements, and compatibility expectations.\n2. Use a concrete case: A mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: A richer API contract can make clients easier to integrate but also creates compatibility and versioning obligations.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define API fundamentals first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use API fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor API fundamentals, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn API is a defined interface through which software components exchange data or trigger operations. A good API contract specifies inputs, outputs, errors, authentication requirements, and compatibility expectations.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define API fundamentals accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: An API is a defined interface through which software components exchange data or trigger operations. A good API contract specifies inputs, outputs, errors, authentication requirements, and compatibility expectations.\n2. Use a concrete case: A mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: A richer API contract can make clients easier to integrate but also creates compatibility and versioning obligations.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define API fundamentals first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use API fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor API fundamentals, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
