    question: "How would you troubleshoot a problem involving API fundamentals?",
    slug: "api-webservice-how-would-you-troubleshoot-a-problem-involving-api-fundamentals-7",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about API fundamentals.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen API fundamentals misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to API fundamentals. An API is a defined interface through which software components exchange data or trigger operations. A good API contract specifies inputs, outputs, errors, authentication requirements, and compatibility expectations.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls API fundamentals: An API is a defined interface through which software components exchange data or trigger operations. A good API contract specifies inputs, outputs, errors, authentication requirements, and compatibility expectations.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A richer API contract can make clients easier to integrate but also creates compatibility and versioning obligations.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use API fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen API fundamentals misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to API fundamentals. An API is a defined interface through which software components exchange data or trigger operations. A good API contract specifies inputs, outputs, errors, authentication requirements, and compatibility expectations.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls API fundamentals: An API is a defined interface through which software components exchange data or trigger operations. A good API contract specifies inputs, outputs, errors, authentication requirements, and compatibility expectations.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A richer API contract can make clients easier to integrate but also creates compatibility and versioning obligations.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use API fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
