    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about API fundamentals.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAPI fundamentals is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. A richer API contract can make clients easier to integrate but also creates compatibility and versioning obligations. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what API fundamentals gives you: An API is a defined interface through which software components exchange data or trigger operations. A good API contract specifies inputs, outputs, errors, authentication requirements, and compatibility expectations.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: A richer API contract can make clients easier to integrate but also creates compatibility and versioning obligations.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use API fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
