    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you evolve API fundamentals without breaking existing clients?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to API fundamentals.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: An API is a defined interface through which software components exchange data or trigger operations. A good API contract specifies inputs, outputs, errors, authentication requirements, and compatibility expectations.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: A richer API contract can make clients easier to integrate but also creates compatibility and versioning obligations.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use API fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a mobile client can call `GET /users/42` and receive a documented JSON representation of user 42, while the server enforces authentication and authorization. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
