    question: "What are the main trade-offs of REST?",
    slug: "api-webservice-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-rest-18",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about REST.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nREST is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. REST does not require every API to follow one exact URL scheme; consistency and correct HTTP semantics matter more than superficial conventions. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what REST gives you: REST is an architectural style for networked systems that commonly uses resources, representations, stateless requests, and standard HTTP semantics.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: REST does not require every API to follow one exact URL scheme; consistency and correct HTTP semantics matter more than superficial conventions.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A client can call `GET /users/42` to retrieve a user resource and receive a documented status code and representation.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA client can call `GET /users/42` to retrieve a user resource and receive a documented status code and representation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use REST when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a client can call `GET /users/42` to retrieve a user resource and receive a documented status code and representation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
