    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTTP is an application-layer request/response protocol used to transfer resources and messages between clients and servers.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define HTTP accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: HTTP is an application-layer request/response protocol used to transfer resources and messages between clients and servers.\n2. Use a concrete case: A client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: HTTP semantics are flexible, but misuse of methods or status codes makes APIs harder to integrate correctly.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define HTTP first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use HTTP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor HTTP, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
