    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for HTTP is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. HTTP is an application-layer request/response protocol used to transfer resources and messages between clients and servers.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: HTTP is an application-layer request/response protocol used to transfer resources and messages between clients and servers.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: HTTP semantics are flexible, but misuse of methods or status codes makes APIs harder to integrate correctly.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use HTTP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
