    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain HTTP in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: HTTP is an application-layer request/response protocol used to transfer resources and messages between clients and servers.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body.\n4. Finish with the limitation: HTTP semantics are flexible, but misuse of methods or status codes makes APIs harder to integrate correctly.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use HTTP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
