    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about HTTP when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to HTTP.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: HTTP is an application-layer request/response protocol used to transfer resources and messages between clients and servers.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: HTTP semantics are flexible, but misuse of methods or status codes makes APIs harder to integrate correctly.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use HTTP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a client sends an HTTP request with a method and headers, and the server returns a status code, headers, and an optional body. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
