    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTTP methods communicate the intended operation, such as `GET` for retrieval, `POST` for creating or processing a request, `PUT` for replacement, `PATCH` for partial modification, and `DELETE` for deletion.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Methods accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: HTTP methods communicate the intended operation, such as `GET` for retrieval, `POST` for creating or processing a request, `PUT` for replacement, `PATCH` for partial modification, and `DELETE` for deletion.\n2. Use a concrete case: A client can use `PATCH /users/42` to change one profile field without sending the entire representation.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Method semantics should match the operation; inventing method meanings makes caching, retries, and client behavior harder to reason about.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Methods first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA client can use `PATCH /users/42` to change one profile field without sending the entire representation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Methods when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a client can use `PATCH /users/42` to change one profile field without sending the entire representation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Methods as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Methods.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Methods, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
