    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Methods comes from the behavior it provides: HTTP methods communicate the intended operation, such as `GET` for retrieval, `POST` for creating or processing a request, `PUT` for replacement, `PATCH` for partial modification, and `DELETE` for deletion. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A client can use `PATCH /users/42` to change one profile field without sending the entire representation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: HTTP methods communicate the intended operation, such as `GET` for retrieval, `POST` for creating or processing a request, `PUT` for replacement, `PATCH` for partial modification, and `DELETE` for deletion.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A client can use `PATCH /users/42` to change one profile field without sending the entire representation.\n3. State the trade-off: Method semantics should match the operation; inventing method meanings makes caching, retries, and client behavior harder to reason about.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA client can use `PATCH /users/42` to change one profile field without sending the entire representation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Methods when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a client can use `PATCH /users/42` to change one profile field without sending the entire representation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Methods as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Methods.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
