    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTTP status codes communicate the broad outcome of a request, including success, client error, and server error classes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Status codes accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: HTTP status codes communicate the broad outcome of a request, including success, client error, and server error classes.\n2. Use a concrete case: A successful resource creation may return `201 Created`, while an invalid request can return `400 Bad Request` or a missing resource `404 Not Found`.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: A status code should describe the protocol-level outcome accurately; application-specific error details belong in the response body when useful.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Status codes first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA successful resource creation may return `201 Created`, while an invalid request can return `400 Bad Request` or a missing resource `404 Not Found`.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Status codes when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a successful resource creation may return `201 Created`, while an invalid request can return `400 Bad Request` or a missing resource `404 Not Found`. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Status codes as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Status codes.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Status codes, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
