    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Status codes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Status codes comes from the behavior it provides: HTTP status codes communicate the broad outcome of a request, including success, client error, and server error classes. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A successful resource creation may return `201 Created`, while an invalid request can return `400 Bad Request` or a missing resource `404 Not Found`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: HTTP status codes communicate the broad outcome of a request, including success, client error, and server error classes.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A successful resource creation may return `201 Created`, while an invalid request can return `400 Bad Request` or a missing resource `404 Not Found`.\n3. State the trade-off: A status code should describe the protocol-level outcome accurately; application-specific error details belong in the response body when useful.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA successful resource creation may return `201 Created`, while an invalid request can return `400 Bad Request` or a missing resource `404 Not Found`.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Status codes when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a successful resource creation may return `201 Created`, while an invalid request can return `400 Bad Request` or a missing resource `404 Not Found`. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Status codes as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Status codes.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
