    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Status codes in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A successful resource creation may return `201 Created`, while an invalid request can return `400 Bad Request` or a missing resource `404 Not Found`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: HTTP status codes communicate the broad outcome of a request, including success, client error, and server error classes.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A successful resource creation may return `201 Created`, while an invalid request can return `400 Bad Request` or a missing resource `404 Not Found`.\n4. Finish with the limitation: A status code should describe the protocol-level outcome accurately; application-specific error details belong in the response body when useful.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA successful resource creation may return `201 Created`, while an invalid request can return `400 Bad Request` or a missing resource `404 Not Found`.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Status codes when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a successful resource creation may return `201 Created`, while an invalid request can return `400 Bad Request` or a missing resource `404 Not Found`. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Status codes as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Status codes.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
