    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTTP headers carry metadata about a request or response, such as content type, authorization, caching, and correlation information.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Headers accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: HTTP headers carry metadata about a request or response, such as content type, authorization, caching, and correlation information.\n2. Use a concrete case: An API can return `Content-Type: application/json` and accept an `Authorization` header from a client.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Headers are part of the protocol contract, so sensitive values should not be logged or exposed casually.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Headers first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAn API can return `Content-Type: application/json` and accept an `Authorization` header from a client.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Headers when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an API can return `Content-Type: application/json` and accept an `Authorization` header from a client. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Headers as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Headers.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Headers, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
