    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Headers.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Headers when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Headers.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: HTTP headers carry metadata about a request or response, such as content type, authorization, caching, and correlation information.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: An API can return `Content-Type: application/json` and accept an `Authorization` header from a client.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Headers are part of the protocol contract, so sensitive values should not be logged or exposed casually.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nAn API can return `Content-Type: application/json` and accept an `Authorization` header from a client.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Headers when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an API can return `Content-Type: application/json` and accept an `Authorization` header from a client. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Headers as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Headers.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
