    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about JSON.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nJSON is a text-based data interchange format commonly used for API request and response bodies.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define JSON accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: JSON is a text-based data interchange format commonly used for API request and response bodies.\n2. Use a concrete case: An endpoint can return `{\"id\":42,\"status\":\"paid\"}` as an `application/json` response.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: JSON is simple and interoperable, but schemas, numeric precision, nullability, and backward compatibility still need deliberate handling.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define JSON first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAn endpoint can return `{\"id\":42,\"status\":\"paid\"}` as an `application/json` response.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use JSON when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an endpoint can return `{\"id\":42,\"status\":\"paid\"}` as an `application/json` response. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating JSON as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with JSON.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor JSON, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
