    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Idempotency.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn idempotent operation produces the same intended server-side effect when the same request is repeated, although individual responses can differ.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Idempotency accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: An idempotent operation produces the same intended server-side effect when the same request is repeated, although individual responses can differ.\n2. Use a concrete case: A payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Idempotency must be designed around the side effect and request identity, not assumed just because a request uses a particular HTTP method.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Idempotency first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Idempotency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Idempotency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Idempotency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Idempotency, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
