    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Idempotency when the problem matches the behavior it provides: An idempotent operation produces the same intended server-side effect when the same request is repeated, although individual responses can differ. A concrete application is A payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Idempotency provides the required behavior: An idempotent operation produces the same intended server-side effect when the same request is repeated, although individual responses can differ.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Idempotency must be designed around the side effect and request identity, not assumed just because a request uses a particular HTTP method.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Idempotency fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Idempotency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Idempotency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Idempotency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Idempotency when the problem matches the behavior it provides: An idempotent operation produces the same intended server-side effect when the same request is repeated, although individual responses can differ. A concrete application is A payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Idempotency provides the required behavior: An idempotent operation produces the same intended server-side effect when the same request is repeated, although individual responses can differ.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Idempotency must be designed around the side effect and request identity, not assumed just because a request uses a particular HTTP method.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Idempotency fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Idempotency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Idempotency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Idempotency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
