    slug: "api-webservice-what-are-the-main-trade-offs-of-idempotency-78",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Idempotency.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIdempotency is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Idempotency must be designed around the side effect and request identity, not assumed just because a request uses a particular HTTP method. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Idempotency gives you: An idempotent operation produces the same intended server-side effect when the same request is repeated, although individual responses can differ.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Idempotency must be designed around the side effect and request identity, not assumed just because a request uses a particular HTTP method.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Idempotency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Idempotency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Idempotency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
