    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Idempotency in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: An idempotent operation produces the same intended server-side effect when the same request is repeated, although individual responses can differ.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Idempotency must be designed around the side effect and request identity, not assumed just because a request uses a particular HTTP method.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Idempotency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Idempotency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Idempotency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
