    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Idempotency.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Idempotency when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Idempotency.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: An idempotent operation produces the same intended server-side effect when the same request is repeated, although individual responses can differ.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Idempotency must be designed around the side effect and request identity, not assumed just because a request uses a particular HTTP method.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Idempotency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment endpoint can accept an idempotency key and return the same logical result when the client retries the same request after a timeout. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Idempotency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Idempotency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
