    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you test or monitor Authentication against an authorization failure or credential compromise?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Authentication.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Authentication verifies the identity associated with a request or credential.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A service can authenticate a request using a validated session or token, then establish the identity that later authorization checks use.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Authentication answers “who are you?”; it does not by itself grant permission to access a resource.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA service can authenticate a request using a validated session or token, then establish the identity that later authorization checks use.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Authentication when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service can authenticate a request using a validated session or token, then establish the identity that later authorization checks use. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you test or monitor Authentication against an authorization failure or credential compromise?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Authentication.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Authentication verifies the identity associated with a request or credential.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A service can authenticate a request using a validated session or token, then establish the identity that later authorization checks use.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Authentication answers “who are you?”; it does not by itself grant permission to access a resource.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA service can authenticate a request using a validated session or token, then establish the identity that later authorization checks use.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Authentication when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service can authenticate a request using a validated session or token, then establish the identity that later authorization checks use. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
