    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Authorization.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAuthorization determines whether an authenticated identity is allowed to perform a specific action on a resource.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Authorization accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Authorization determines whether an authenticated identity is allowed to perform a specific action on a resource.\n2. Use a concrete case: A user may be authenticated but still receive `403 Forbidden` when attempting to modify another customer’s order.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Authorization rules need explicit resource and action checks; trusting client-provided roles is unsafe.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Authorization first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA user may be authenticated but still receive `403 Forbidden` when attempting to modify another customer’s order.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Authorization when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a user may be authenticated but still receive `403 Forbidden` when attempting to modify another customer’s order. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Authorization, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
