    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Authorization misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Authorization. Authorization determines whether an authenticated identity is allowed to perform a specific action on a resource.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Authorization: Authorization determines whether an authenticated identity is allowed to perform a specific action on a resource.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A user may be authenticated but still receive `403 Forbidden` when attempting to modify another customer’s order.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Authorization rules need explicit resource and action checks; trusting client-provided roles is unsafe.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA user may be authenticated but still receive `403 Forbidden` when attempting to modify another customer’s order.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Authorization when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a user may be authenticated but still receive `403 Forbidden` when attempting to modify another customer’s order. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
