    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about OAuth.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nOAuth is an authorization framework for delegated access in which a client obtains access tokens to call protected resources on behalf of a resource owner.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define OAuth accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: OAuth is an authorization framework for delegated access in which a client obtains access tokens to call protected resources on behalf of a resource owner.\n2. Use a concrete case: A web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: OAuth defines authorization mechanisms; it is not itself an identity protocol, which is where OIDC is used.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define OAuth first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use OAuth when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor OAuth, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
