    question: "What are the main benefits of OAuth?",
    slug: "api-webservice-what-are-the-main-benefits-of-oauth-104",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about OAuth.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of OAuth comes from the behavior it provides: OAuth is an authorization framework for delegated access in which a client obtains access tokens to call protected resources on behalf of a resource owner. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: OAuth is an authorization framework for delegated access in which a client obtains access tokens to call protected resources on behalf of a resource owner.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n3. State the trade-off: OAuth defines authorization mechanisms; it is not itself an identity protocol, which is where OIDC is used.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use OAuth when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
