    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about OAuth.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen OAuth misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to OAuth. OAuth is an authorization framework for delegated access in which a client obtains access tokens to call protected resources on behalf of a resource owner.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls OAuth: OAuth is an authorization framework for delegated access in which a client obtains access tokens to call protected resources on behalf of a resource owner.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: OAuth defines authorization mechanisms; it is not itself an identity protocol, which is where OIDC is used.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use OAuth when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
