    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nOAuth is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. OAuth defines authorization mechanisms; it is not itself an identity protocol, which is where OIDC is used. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what OAuth gives you: OAuth is an authorization framework for delegated access in which a client obtains access tokens to call protected resources on behalf of a resource owner.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: OAuth defines authorization mechanisms; it is not itself an identity protocol, which is where OIDC is used.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use OAuth when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
