    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about OAuth.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain OAuth in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: A web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: OAuth is an authorization framework for delegated access in which a client obtains access tokens to call protected resources on behalf of a resource owner.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: A web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n4. Finish with the limitation: OAuth defines authorization mechanisms; it is not itself an identity protocol, which is where OIDC is used.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nA web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use OAuth when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
