    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about OAuth when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to OAuth.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: OAuth is an authorization framework for delegated access in which a client obtains access tokens to call protected resources on behalf of a resource owner.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: OAuth defines authorization mechanisms; it is not itself an identity protocol, which is where OIDC is used.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use OAuth when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a web application can obtain an access token after an authorization flow and use it to call a user’s permitted API resources. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
