    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about OIDC.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of OIDC comes from the behavior it provides: OpenID Connect extends OAuth 2.0 with an identity layer that lets a client verify a user identity and obtain identity claims. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A login flow can return an ID token containing claims about the authenticated user after the authorization server authenticates them.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: OpenID Connect extends OAuth 2.0 with an identity layer that lets a client verify a user identity and obtain identity claims.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A login flow can return an ID token containing claims about the authenticated user after the authorization server authenticates them.\n3. State the trade-off: An ID token is intended for the client as an identity artifact; it should not be confused with an API access token.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA login flow can return an ID token containing claims about the authenticated user after the authorization server authenticates them.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use OIDC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a login flow can return an ID token containing claims about the authenticated user after the authorization server authenticates them. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating OIDC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with OIDC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of OIDC comes from the behavior it provides: OpenID Connect extends OAuth 2.0 with an identity layer that lets a client verify a user identity and obtain identity claims. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A login flow can return an ID token containing claims about the authenticated user after the authorization server authenticates them.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: OpenID Connect extends OAuth 2.0 with an identity layer that lets a client verify a user identity and obtain identity claims.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A login flow can return an ID token containing claims about the authenticated user after the authorization server authenticates them.\n3. State the trade-off: An ID token is intended for the client as an identity artifact; it should not be confused with an API access token.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA login flow can return an ID token containing claims about the authenticated user after the authorization server authenticates them.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use OIDC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a login flow can return an ID token containing claims about the authenticated user after the authorization server authenticates them. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating OIDC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with OIDC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
