    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen OIDC misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to OIDC. OpenID Connect extends OAuth 2.0 with an identity layer that lets a client verify a user identity and obtain identity claims.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls OIDC: OpenID Connect extends OAuth 2.0 with an identity layer that lets a client verify a user identity and obtain identity claims.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A login flow can return an ID token containing claims about the authenticated user after the authorization server authenticates them.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: An ID token is intended for the client as an identity artifact; it should not be confused with an API access token.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA login flow can return an ID token containing claims about the authenticated user after the authorization server authenticates them.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use OIDC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a login flow can return an ID token containing claims about the authenticated user after the authorization server authenticates them. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating OIDC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with OIDC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
